The historical terms \emph{renormalizable} and \emph{non-renormalizable} refer
to properties of the perturbative theories. We will make this distinction
precise later.

\subsection{Plan of Physics 253a}
\label{sec:253a-plan}

The course plan is:
\begin{enumerate}
  \setlength{\itemsep}{2pt}
  \setlength{\parsep}{0pt}
  \setlength{\parskip}{0pt}
  \item Lagrangian formulation of quantum mechanics:
    \begin{itemize}
      \setlength{\itemsep}{1pt}
      \setlength{\parsep}{0pt}
      \setlength{\topsep}{2pt}
      \item path integral and regularization;
      \item perturbation theory and Feynman diagrams;
      \item renormalization and counter terms.
    \end{itemize}
  \item Relativistic particles and fields:
    \begin{itemize}
      \setlength{\itemsep}{1pt}
      \setlength{\parsep}{0pt}
      \setlength{\topsep}{2pt}
      \item $\phi^4$ theory;
      \item Green functions;
      \item asymptotic states;
      \item S-matrix and LSZ reduction.
    \end{itemize}
  \item Particles and fields with spin:
    \begin{itemize}
      \setlength{\itemsep}{1pt}
      \setlength{\parsep}{0pt}
      \setlength{\topsep}{2pt}
      \item classification of relativistic particles;
      \item fermions and gauge bosons;
      \item QED;
      \item applications to the electron $g$-factor and the Lamb shift.
    \end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}

Canonical quantization enters before the construction of scalar field theory.

\subsection{Relativistic particles without fields}
\label{sec:253a-relativistic-particles}

Let us try. As in any quantum-mechanical system, we have a Hilbert space whose
vectors correspond to physical states. There will probably be a vacuum state;
let us call it $|\Omega\rangle$.

\begin{equation}
  H\ket{\Omega}=0.
  \label{eq:253a-vacuum-energy}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
  H\ket{\mathbf p}
  =\sqrt{\mathbf p^{\,2}+m^2}\,\ket{\mathbf p}.
  \label{eq:253a-one-particle-energy}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
  H\ket{\mathbf p_1,\ldots,\mathbf p_n}
  =\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sqrt{\mathbf p_i^{\,2}+m^2}\right)
   \ket{\mathbf p_1,\ldots,\mathbf p_n}.
  \label{eq:253a-multiparticle-energy}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
  \ket{\mathbf p}=a_{\mathbf p}^{+}\ket{\Omega},
  \qquad
  \ket{\mathbf p_1,\mathbf p_2}
  =a_{\mathbf p_1}^{+}a_{\mathbf p_2}^{+}\ket{\Omega},
  \qquad \text{etc.}
  \label{eq:253a-created-states}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
  [a,a]=[a^{+},a^{+}]=0,
  \qquad
  [a_{\mathbf p},a_{\mathbf p'}^{+}]
  =\delta^{(D-1)}(\mathbf p-\mathbf p'),
  \qquad
  \braket{\mathbf p}{\mathbf p'}
  =\delta^{(D-1)}(\mathbf p-\mathbf p').
  \label{eq:253a-oscillator-normalization}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
  H=\int d^{D-1}\mathbf p\,
  \sqrt{\mathbf p^{\,2}+m^2}\,
  a_{\mathbf p}^{+}a_{\mathbf p}.
  \label{eq:253a-free-hamiltonian}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
  H=H_0+
  \underbrace{H_{\mathrm{int}}}_{
    a^{+}a^{+}a,\;aaa^{+},\;\ldots}\, .
  \label{eq:253a-interaction-examples}
\end{equation}

The difficulty is preserving relativistic symmetry. It is possible to find an
$H_{\mathrm{int}}$ that respects it, but it is not easy.

